  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion testable="true">Given task 001's resolved spike note exists, when ADR-047 concise is authored, then its delivery-mode section cites the spike note's decision by file path and states the chosen mode (or the revised out-of-BFF placement if >60 MB was breached).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Given the six architectural commitments, when ADR-047 concise's Decision/Constraints sections are read, then all six (typed signals, shared domain actions, per-source policy, SSE-as-presentation, outbox-before-ping, dumb-transport) are each stated as an explicit MUST or MUST NOT rule.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Given `.claude/adr/ADR-047-notification-action-spine.md`, when its line count is checked, then it is within the 100-150 line target stated by `.claude/adr/INDEX.md`.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Given `docs/adr/ADR-047-notification-action-spine.md`, when read, then it contains a Context section explaining the fork-prevention rationale (email-r4/messaging-r3/assistant-r1 each independently designing push) and links back to the concise version.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Given `.claude/adr/INDEX.md`, when inspected after this task, then it contains an ADR-047 row AND the ADR-046/ADR-048 rows no longer describe ADR-047 as merely "reserved."</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Given `.claude/CHANGELOG.md`, when inspected after this task, then its `[Unreleased]` section has an Added entry citing both new ADR-047 files.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Given the spike is unresolved (negative case), when this task is attempted, then it does NOT fabricate a mode decision — it fires the escalation trigger instead.</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>
  <notes>Spec FR-18. Reflect the ADR Tensions table already resolved in spec.md (ADR-043 Path C — comply, sequenced via the "what lights up" audit) rather than re-deciding it; ADR-047 documents that resolution, it does not reopen it.</notes>
  <justification>
    <existing>ADR-043 (dispatch/execution spine) and ADR-045 (communication architecture) are the nearest ADRs — both adjacent (dispatch re-entry, communication persistence) but neither covers a cross-project delivery spine (typed signal → grounded action → push/poll delivery). Verified by grep: no ADR mentions "outbox" as a durable pending-signal store or "SignalR" as a delivery mechanism.</existing>
    <extension>No — this is a new capability class (a shared delivery spine), not an extension of dispatch or communication architecture; ADR-046/ADR-048 already reserve the number specifically because their authors recognized it as a distinct, adjacent decision.</extension>
    <cost-of-doing-nothing>Without this ADR, the next project needing server-initiated push (a fourth consumer beyond the three this project already unifies) has no documented decision to build against and re-litigates spine ownership from scratch — the exact fork risk root CLAUDE.md §6/§11 exists to prevent, and the exact failure mode spec §Executive Summary names explicitly ("no second push/delivery mechanism").</cost-of-doing-nothing>
  </justification>
